00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:04.740
Picture a world the size of Neptune, sitting so close to its star that a round-trip light

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.780
signal would take less than a day to complete.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:11.080
The same distance a commercial jet covers between Vancouver and Tokyo.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:17.960
Standard Formation Theory says giant planets must grow far from their stars where ice can help build solid cores.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:24.260
Then migrate inward. Yet this backward orbiting Neptune appears to have arrived in the wrong direction

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:30.800
without leaving the usual trail of aligned companions. The mechanism involves early disk interactions that

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:36.960
can flip orbital direction when a planet scatters off a sibling or when the disc itself warps under uneven

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:42.780
magnetic torques. One concrete measurement shows the planet completes its reversed orbit in roughly

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:50.760
11 days at a separation of only 0.06 astronomical units. A distance where temperatures exceed 1,000

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:56.560
degrees and any original atmosphere should have been stripped. We can map the orbit from repeated

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:02.580
transit and radial velocity data. But nobody knows why the migration reversed instead of simply

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.700
shrinking the orbit while keeping the original direction. The unanswered question is whether

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:13.380
such flipped giants are common or whether we are seeing the rare survivors of chaotic early system

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:19.420
